<h2>The road not taken: Najeeb delay</h2>
<p>UAPA bail jurisprudence has two escape valves from Section 43D(5)&rsquo;s embargo: showing the accusations are not prima facie true (Watali), and showing that prolonged incarceration with no end in sight violates Article 21 (K.A. Najeeb). The Bench closed both conventional doors — the accusations were prima facie true, and with the trial expected to conclude within six to eight months, the delay argument lacked its factual premise. What remained was the statute itself.</p>

<h2>Section 479 BNSS: the mechanics</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>Rule</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 479(1) BNSS</td><td>An undertrial who has undergone up to one-half of the maximum imprisonment for the offence (not being one punishable with death or life) shall be released on bail</td></tr>
  <tr><td>First proviso</td><td>A first-time offender qualifies at one-third of the maximum period</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 479(2)</td><td>No release under the section where investigation, inquiry or trial is pending in more than one offence or in multiple cases — the bar the Court read down to a case-by-case discretion</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 436-A CrPC</td><td>The predecessor provision, applicable to custody periods accrued under the old Code — relevant since the arrests predated the BNSS</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Because the order on charge confined the case to Sections 120B IPC, 13, 38 and 39 UAPA — with ten years as the highest maximum — the arithmetic favoured both appellants. The Court tabulated the status of all eleven accused: one dead, two on default bail since 2023, several on regular bail through 2022–2026, one discharged. The appellants were, by late 2026, among the last in custody.</p>

<h2>The conditions regime</h2>
<p>Bail came tightly wrapped: bonds of Rs 2 lakh (Waqar Lone) and Rs 5 lakh (Deepthi Marla) with two sureties each; current addresses and always-on contact numbers to the IO and trial court with surprise-check liberty; weekly Monday reporting to the local SHO with monthly written reports to the NIA; no travel beyond set territorial limits without leave; passport surrender with immigration authorities alerted; no contact with witnesses; no statements, posts or publications about the case till the trial concludes; no attendance at rallies or gatherings; and no online activity supporting ISIS or any terrorist organisation — with cancellation available on breach or on dilatory conduct at trial.</p>

<h2>Why the judgment matters beyond its facts</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>It operationalises Section 479 in special statutes.</strong> The holding that the BNSS undertrial cap reaches UAPA prosecutions gives defence counsel a statutory, arithmetic route that does not depend on persuading a court to disbelieve the NIA&rsquo;s case.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>It softens Section 479(2).</strong> Reading the multiple-case bar as discretionary rather than mandatory prevents stale or concluded involvements from indefinitely defeating the cap.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>It disciplines charge-stage realism.</strong> The order on charge — sympathisers, no violent act — set the sentencing ceiling that made the arithmetic work. Charge-framing outcomes have long bail shadows.</div>
</div>
